1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:05,073
Can a demonstration guide a robot through a long-horizon task?

2
00:00:05,373 --> 00:00:09,320
RoboDemo separates two things worth remembering.

3
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:13,304
The intent in the demonstration, and the progress actually made.

4
00:00:14,115 --> 00:00:19,260
An instruction may leave the grasp, motion, and order underspecified.

5
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:24,937
A reference shows a procedure, not the robot's completed progress.

6
00:00:25,237 --> 00:00:30,198
We need both a reference and memory that updates with interaction.

7
00:00:31,032 --> 00:00:33,779
A human demonstration can be supplied directly.

8
00:00:34,079 --> 00:00:39,121
Or retrieve a robot demonstration using the initial image and instruction.

9
00:00:39,421 --> 00:00:42,352
Retrieval never uses future frames of the current query trajectory.

10
00:00:42,652 --> 00:00:47,184
Retrieved-reference policy-control results have not been reported.

11
00:00:48,032 --> 00:00:53,133
The current design uses one reference segment, not multiple segments.

12
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:57,222
Two complementary sets of frames come from that same segment.

13
00:00:57,522 --> 00:01:01,936
Sparse semantic frames capture objects, scene context, and meaning.

14
00:01:02,236 --> 00:01:06,215
A dense consecutive slice preserves local changes in motion.

15
00:01:07,032 --> 00:01:10,064
Semantic frames enter the Qwen3-VL vision tower.

16
00:01:10,364 --> 00:01:14,397
The consecutive action slice enters the V-JEPA2 encoder.

17
00:01:14,697 --> 00:01:19,902
Both encoders are frozen; images become groups of tokens.

18
00:01:20,202 --> 00:01:26,585
Separate Q-formers pool them: a semantic base plus gated motion residual.

19
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:31,202
This context is not simply stored in a video cache.

20
00:01:31,502 --> 00:01:37,877
Context K and V update a small fast-weight learner through an inner objective.

21
00:01:38,177 --> 00:01:42,268
Action-side Q queries the memory; it does not write.

22
00:01:42,568 --> 00:01:47,036
The fast state changes, not the entire policy network.

23
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:52,908
Purple reference memory is preloaded, then remains fixed throughout rollout.

24
00:01:53,208 --> 00:01:57,461
Pink progress memory waits for an actual transition to finish.

25
00:01:57,761 --> 00:02:02,890
It receives the new observation, robot state, and preceding executed action.

26
00:02:03,190 --> 00:02:08,847
Progress keeps updating without overwriting the reference intent.

27
00:02:09,657 --> 00:02:12,993
Each action block first reads the demonstration intent.

28
00:02:13,293 --> 00:02:18,376
Progress then refines that reference-conditioned action representation.

29
00:02:18,676 --> 00:02:22,414
The action representation changes, not the reference memory.

30
00:02:22,714 --> 00:02:28,584
Both states are read-only during denoising; an unwritten state adds zero delta.

31
00:02:29,407 --> 00:02:33,607
At the historical 240k checkpoint with human references,

32
00:02:33,907 --> 00:02:37,928
50 tasks across four levels total 2,000 trials.

33
00:02:38,228 --> 00:02:44,304
RoboDemo succeeds at least once in 1,692 trials: 84.6%.

34
00:02:44,604 --> 00:02:49,177
The aggregate exceeds the listed baselines, not every individual task.

35
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:55,585
These are recorded simulated policy executions, not generated robot footage.

36
00:02:55,885 --> 00:03:01,927
Across six selected cases: 6/6 with reference, 0/6 without.

37
00:03:02,227 --> 00:03:08,117
This is not a full-benchmark ablation; static and reversed references also succeed.

38
00:03:08,417 --> 00:03:12,972
These cases do not establish that full temporal order is necessary.

39
00:03:15,073 --> 00:03:19,199
We also compare representations of the same 309 human videos.

40
00:03:19,499 --> 00:03:23,207
Appearance control is on the left; fast weights are on the right.

41
00:03:23,507 --> 00:03:28,761
Task structure is visible, but clustering is not causal proof of action understanding.

42
00:03:29,061 --> 00:03:32,140
Evidence for general human–robot alignment also remains limited.

43
00:03:32,990 --> 00:03:35,514
Finally, keep two timelines distinct.

44
00:03:35,814 --> 00:03:42,041
The current method uses one segment; the historical checkpoint used four.

45
00:03:42,341 --> 00:03:46,270
Historical results do not automatically validate the revised design.

46
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:52,201
84.6% is first success, not success at the fixed 500-step endpoint.

47
00:03:53,032 --> 00:03:58,046
RoboDemo is not about blindly replaying a demonstration.

48
00:03:58,346 --> 00:04:02,693
Remember what should happen, and what actually happened.

49
00:04:02,993 --> 00:04:07,677
Let intent and actual progress guide the next action together.
